1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,869
Look down the next time you step out your
door.

2
00:00:02,101 --> 00:00:03,101
There's a road under you.

3
00:00:03,297 --> 00:00:07,326
You use roads so often that you never ask
where they come from — you save that for

4
00:00:07,326 --> 00:00:09,636
old things, and roads don't feel old to
you.

5
00:00:10,008 --> 00:00:12,319
They feel like they belong to cars,
trucks, freight.

6
00:00:12,899 --> 00:00:14,571
So who built the first roads, and why?

7
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:16,696
You'll probably reach for one of three.

8
00:00:16,847 --> 00:00:18,925
The Romans. Or traders, moving goods.

9
00:00:19,134 --> 00:00:21,084
Or no one, until someone had a wheel.

10
00:00:21,583 --> 00:00:24,764
All three feel right. All three, as far as
we can tell, are wrong.

11
00:00:25,089 --> 00:00:27,493
The earliest road builders we can name
weren't fighting distance.

12
00:00:27,725 --> 00:00:28,725
They were fighting water.

13
00:00:29,188 --> 00:00:32,694
Their land was going soft under their
feet, so they built it a floor.

14
00:00:33,114 --> 00:00:35,401
To see what that means, you need to stand
in a bog.

15
00:00:35,726 --> 00:00:37,503
A bog is land that never dries.

16
00:00:37,735 --> 00:00:42,007
Rain sits on dead moss, new moss grows
over it, and the ground turns into a wet

17
00:00:42,007 --> 00:00:44,817
sponge — it will take your boot, and it
can take you.

18
00:00:44,968 --> 00:00:46,524
You can't farm it. You can't cross it.

19
00:00:46,895 --> 00:00:50,390
Almost six thousand years ago, in what's
now Somerset in the south of England,

20
00:00:50,390 --> 00:00:53,641
people faced the same ground you'd sink in
— and they built.

21
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:55,709
We call what they built the Sweet Track.

22
00:00:55,976 --> 00:01:00,759
Tree rings pin its date — each year a tree
adds one ring, wide in a good year, thin

23
00:01:00,759 --> 00:01:03,940
in a bad one, and you can match that
pattern against a known series.

24
00:01:04,172 --> 00:01:08,329
The result: 3807 BC — or 3806, because the
trees

25
00:01:08,329 --> 00:01:12,230
were felled in winter, one winter spans
two calendar years, and even the wood

26
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:13,472
tell you which.

27
00:01:13,892 --> 00:01:15,506
And what stood there was no trail.

28
00:01:15,831 --> 00:01:16,710
It was a structure.

29
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:20,370
About 1,800 metres of walkway across a
reed marsh, from an island at

30
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:22,042
Westhay to a ridge at Shapwick.

31
00:01:22,623 --> 00:01:26,384
Oak planks up to 40 centimetres wide,
three metres long, less than five

32
00:01:26,384 --> 00:01:30,529
thick — split, not sawn, out of oaks up to
400 years old and a metre through.

33
00:01:31,109 --> 00:01:33,884
The tool kit: stone axes, wooden wedges,
wooden mallets.

34
00:01:34,209 --> 00:01:35,007
That's all.

35
00:01:35,057 --> 00:01:38,644
They drove crossed pegs of ash, oak and
lime into the peat — the bog's packed dead

36
00:01:38,644 --> 00:01:42,580
plants — laid long rails of hazel and
alder, cut notches into the planks, and

37
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:43,637
slotted the parts together.

38
00:01:43,869 --> 00:01:45,401
No nails. A push fit.

39
00:01:45,831 --> 00:01:48,978
And the pegs are so straight and clean
that the wood points to a managed stand —

40
00:01:48,978 --> 00:01:53,598
trees cut low and regrown on cycles for at
least 120 years before this was built.

41
00:01:54,018 --> 00:01:55,527
Now hold on to the strangest part.

42
00:01:56,027 --> 00:01:59,730
All that effort bought a walkway of single
oak planks, laid end to end.

43
00:01:59,997 --> 00:02:02,435
Not a cart track — a plank line through a
marsh.

44
00:02:02,934 --> 00:02:04,351
It served for about ten years.

45
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:05,942
Then the water rose again and took it.

46
00:02:06,441 --> 00:02:09,738
And under it runs an older line still, the
Post Track, from around

47
00:02:09,738 --> 00:02:12,838
3838 BC — same route, thirty years
earlier.

48
00:02:13,163 --> 00:02:14,731
They had done this before.

49
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:16,415
One site might be a fluke.

50
00:02:16,485 --> 00:02:19,957
So go north-east, to the flat wet country
of Lower Saxony in northern Germany.

51
00:02:20,456 --> 00:02:25,112
More than 550 wooden paths are known
there, laid across bogs and soaked ground,

52
00:02:25,112 --> 00:02:26,691
from the Stone Age into modern times.

53
00:02:27,016 --> 00:02:30,197
And beside one of those paths, diggers
found a thing that had no business being

54
00:02:30,197 --> 00:02:33,250
there. Hold on to that — we'll come back
for it.

55
00:02:34,052 --> 00:02:36,246
Some of the oldest paths sit near a lake
called the Dümmer.

56
00:02:36,617 --> 00:02:40,669
One of them, a path labeled Pr 31, crossed
a bog called the Campemoor.

57
00:02:40,878 --> 00:02:44,059
It's around six and a half thousand years
old — the studies differ on the exact

58
00:02:44,059 --> 00:02:45,998
years, so hold that number loosely.

59
00:02:46,265 --> 00:02:47,971
It ran up to four and a half metres wide.

60
00:02:48,296 --> 00:02:51,373
And it had layers: thin birch trunks at
the bottom, three rows of pine logs as a

61
00:02:51,373 --> 00:02:55,390
base, a surface of pine logs up to 20
centimetres thick, pinned along the edges

62
00:02:55,390 --> 00:02:58,362
with birch pegs. Base, bed, surface.

63
00:02:58,792 --> 00:03:01,044
That is road engineering — done with stone
axes.

64
00:03:01,253 --> 00:03:03,134
The chop marks are still on the ends of
the logs.

65
00:03:03,366 --> 00:03:07,360
And thirty metres to one side, there runs
a second path, laid roughly seventeen

66
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:11,063
hundred years later. Same bog, same
problem, same answer.

67
00:03:11,484 --> 00:03:12,935
Why there, why then?

68
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:17,057
The team behind the newest study says it
with care — it's one paper, and they call

69
00:03:17,057 --> 00:03:21,504
their reading a hypothesis — but it goes
like this: the rain grew, the lake rose,

70
00:03:21,504 --> 00:03:23,570
the wet spread, and the paths follow that
curve.

71
00:03:23,942 --> 00:03:27,402
Pr 31, they write, was created in a phase
of increasing waterlogging.

72
00:03:27,576 --> 00:03:31,686
They point to a second driver too — people
were settling in new patterns — so even

73
00:03:31,686 --> 00:03:33,415
here, water isn't the whole story.

74
00:03:33,915 --> 00:03:37,630
But zoom out, and the paths seem to come
in waves: building booms that track the

75
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:38,630
ground getting worse.

76
00:03:38,977 --> 00:03:42,936
One more trace, and it's an odd one:
pollen from the bog shows the pine stands

77
00:03:42,936 --> 00:03:46,616
shrinking after that second path was built
— the likely cause is the wood the

78
00:03:46,616 --> 00:03:50,308
builders took. A forest, shrinking,
because a road was being built.

79
00:03:50,728 --> 00:03:53,120
Now, the thing that had no business being
there.

80
00:03:53,619 --> 00:03:58,077
By one of these paths — Pr 7, laid in the
25th century BC — diggers

81
00:03:58,077 --> 00:04:00,283
found it: two broken wagon axles.

82
00:04:00,713 --> 00:04:03,848
They count among the oldest evidence of
wheels and wagons in all of northern

83
00:04:03,848 --> 00:04:05,462
Germany. So run the numbers with me.

84
00:04:05,833 --> 00:04:09,154
Oldest known road in that region: about
six and a half thousand years old.

85
00:04:09,839 --> 00:04:12,114
Oldest known wheels: about four and a half
thousand.

86
00:04:12,544 --> 00:04:14,378
The road wins by roughly two thousand
years.

87
00:04:14,877 --> 00:04:18,116
People there built roads for some twenty
centuries before anything rolled on one.

88
00:04:18,696 --> 00:04:21,692
One axle lay broken next to the path,
right there in the bog.

89
00:04:22,272 --> 00:04:23,735
The wheel is not the parent of the road.

90
00:04:24,234 --> 00:04:25,836
The wheel is a guest on it.

91
00:04:26,256 --> 00:04:30,180
So if the first roads weren't for wheels,
and weren't for goods — what were roads

92
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:31,152
for?

93
00:04:31,202 --> 00:04:35,544
Chase that question around the world, and
you get a new answer at every stop — and

94
00:04:35,544 --> 00:04:37,634
the answers are stranger than you'd think.

95
00:04:38,054 --> 00:04:40,318
Start in Egypt, about 4,500 years ago.

96
00:04:40,689 --> 00:04:44,474
In the desert south-west of what's now
Cairo, a paved road runs some eleven

97
00:04:44,474 --> 00:04:48,874
kilometres — the reports differ a little
on the length — from a quarry, a pit where

98
00:04:48,874 --> 00:04:51,335
you cut stone, down to what was then the
shore of a lake.

99
00:04:51,765 --> 00:04:56,223
The quarry is called Widan el-Faras, and
the paving is whatever lay close:

100
00:04:56,223 --> 00:05:01,901
basalt, a hard dark lava stone; limestone;
sandstone; and slabs of petrified wood —

101
00:05:01,901 --> 00:05:04,142
dead trees turned to stone, laid as
pavement.

102
00:05:04,722 --> 00:05:06,464
The road's job? No market anywhere.

103
00:05:07,044 --> 00:05:10,179
Crews hauled basalt blocks down it to the
water and shipped them off to become

104
00:05:10,179 --> 00:05:13,790
temple floors for the pyramids — one of
those floors sits just east of the Great

105
00:05:13,790 --> 00:05:17,516
Pyramid at Giza. Even here, the road was
only the hard leg of the trip.

106
00:05:18,097 --> 00:05:21,673
The blocks moved mostly by boat, in
seasons when the lake stood high.

107
00:05:22,102 --> 00:05:24,773
This road was a supply drive for a royal
building site.

108
00:05:25,193 --> 00:05:30,034
Walls of glazed brick, and on them lions,
bulls and dragons — about 120 figures

109
00:05:30,034 --> 00:05:32,798
glowing on yellow and black, lining a
street in Babylon.

110
00:05:33,169 --> 00:05:37,198
This is in what's now Iraq, around 569 BC,
under King

111
00:05:37,198 --> 00:05:38,533
Nebuchadnezzar the Second.

112
00:05:39,334 --> 00:05:43,607
The street itself is stone slabs set in a
bed of bitumen — natural tar — up to

113
00:05:43,607 --> 00:05:45,139
twenty metres wide at some points.

114
00:05:45,569 --> 00:05:49,145
Picture yourself walking it: from the
river through the temple district, past

115
00:05:49,145 --> 00:05:52,338
palaces, to the Ishtar Gate — the city's
great blue gate.

116
00:05:52,547 --> 00:05:55,438
People used it all year; it was the main
axis of the city.

117
00:05:55,809 --> 00:05:59,652
But the day it was dressed for came once a
year: at the spring new year, statues of

118
00:05:59,652 --> 00:06:02,752
the gods were carried through the gate and
down this street in a grand procession.

119
00:06:03,251 --> 00:06:07,547
All of it — the tar, the lions, the glaze
— was, at heart, a stage.

120
00:06:07,966 --> 00:06:13,516
Persia — roughly, modern Iran — gets one
breath: a road of about 2,700 kilometres,

121
00:06:13,516 --> 00:06:18,345
Susa to Sardis, ridden in nine days with
fresh horses waiting along the way,

122
00:06:18,345 --> 00:06:20,691
walked in ninety. A post road, the record
says.

123
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:23,013
It moved messages. It ran an empire.

124
00:06:23,432 --> 00:06:25,185
And then there's the road nobody can
explain.

125
00:06:25,870 --> 00:06:30,572
Chaco Canyon, New Mexico, in the dry
south-west of the United States — roughly

126
00:06:30,572 --> 00:06:33,068
thousand years back, though the dating is
argued over.

127
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:35,437
The people there had no wheel, no horse,
no ox.

128
00:06:35,611 --> 00:06:39,361
Yet they scraped road beds into the desert
floor up to nine metres wide, dead

129
00:06:39,361 --> 00:06:42,147
straight — one of them runs 50.5
kilometres.

130
00:06:42,647 --> 00:06:46,199
Where a cliff stood in the way, they cut
stone stairs into the rock face and went

131
00:06:46,199 --> 00:06:47,871
straight over. Nothing ever rolled here.

132
00:06:48,196 --> 00:06:49,438
So why the width, why the line?

133
00:06:49,566 --> 00:06:51,052
The honest answer: nobody knows.

134
00:06:51,377 --> 00:06:54,512
Scholars have argued for decades — work,
war, worship, politics.

135
00:06:54,942 --> 00:06:58,576
A recent laser survey found roads lined up
on the midwinter sunrise over a mountain

136
00:06:58,576 --> 00:07:02,895
sacred to the Native Pueblo peoples today
— a hint toward ritual, and still one

137
00:07:02,895 --> 00:07:05,913
reading among several. We can map these
roads from the air.

138
00:07:06,285 --> 00:07:08,247
We can't tell you what they were for.

139
00:07:08,667 --> 00:07:12,150
Last stop: the Andes, the long mountain
spine of South America.

140
00:07:12,730 --> 00:07:17,038
The Inca empire ran a road net whose true
size nobody can state — estimates go from

141
00:07:17,038 --> 00:07:19,186
23,000 to 60,000 kilometres.

142
00:07:19,511 --> 00:07:23,029
Parts are older than the empire, laid by
earlier peoples, the Wari and the

143
00:07:23,029 --> 00:07:24,029
Tiwanaku.

144
00:07:24,283 --> 00:07:28,242
It was paved only where paving earned its
keep; elsewhere it was packed earth and

145
00:07:28,242 --> 00:07:28,749
sand.

146
00:07:28,799 --> 00:07:32,387
Its builders had no wheel and no animal to
pull a cart, so where the land climbed,

147
00:07:32,387 --> 00:07:35,312
the road did what no cart road can do: it
became a staircase.

148
00:07:35,545 --> 00:07:36,775
Stairs stop a wheel cold.

149
00:07:37,042 --> 00:07:39,596
For you, on foot, they are simply the
shortest way up.

150
00:07:39,968 --> 00:07:43,776
On this net, an empire moved on foot — and
today, only about a quarter of it is

151
00:07:43,776 --> 00:07:45,169
still visible at all.

152
00:07:45,589 --> 00:07:48,132
Which brings us, at the very end, to trade
— and to Rome.

153
00:07:48,712 --> 00:07:51,835
You were promised the Romans; here they
are, in last place.

154
00:07:52,636 --> 00:07:56,909
Rome's first great road, the Appian Way,
was begun in 312 BC as a

155
00:07:56,909 --> 00:07:59,858
military road, in the middle of a war, to
move troops south.

156
00:07:59,951 --> 00:08:04,409
Not a trade route. And the picture-book
Roman road, that deep stack of stone

157
00:08:04,989 --> 00:08:09,018
Even in Rome it was the top class, not the
rule: Roman law sorted roads into three

158
00:08:09,018 --> 00:08:11,688
kinds, and two of them were plain earth
and gravel.

159
00:08:12,108 --> 00:08:16,160
Trade is the answer you'd expect — and the
one that keeps not showing up.

160
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:18,993
That doesn't mean traders never walked
these paths.

161
00:08:19,678 --> 00:08:23,416
It means trade moved in later, like the
wheel did, into a thing that was already

162
00:08:23,416 --> 00:08:25,995
old. So here's the thought to keep.

163
00:08:26,169 --> 00:08:27,981
A road was never only about speed.

164
00:08:28,782 --> 00:08:32,799
A road is a promise: the ground will hold,
and the way will still be there tomorrow

165
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:34,668
— in the wet season, in the dark.

166
00:08:35,167 --> 00:08:38,824
People in a Somerset marsh made that
promise with stone axes, almost six

167
00:08:38,824 --> 00:08:41,576
years back, and kept it through ten years
of rising water.

168
00:08:42,075 --> 00:08:45,639
And in that German bog country, the oldest
road and the oldest wheel we've found

169
00:08:45,639 --> 00:08:47,996
lie about two thousand years apart — road
first.

170
00:08:48,577 --> 00:08:51,421
So the next time the ground under you goes
soft, you know what humans do.

171
00:08:52,002 --> 00:08:53,198
They did it before the wheel.

172
00:08:53,627 --> 00:08:54,684
They did it before Rome.

173
00:08:55,264 --> 00:08:58,318
They look at land that says no — and they
build a yes.
